    "message": "feat(admin): make the Web Search view answer which route is live\n\nFourteen provider cards competed equally for attention and the one genuinely\ndiagnostic fact on the page -- what the last real request actually did -- was\na flat muted line with the same weight as everything else.\n\nThe route summary is now a hero: an eyebrow, the effective provider set\nlarge, and the route path below it. The path is segmented rather than a flat\nstring, so the primary hop reads at full contrast and every fallback after\nthe first arrow drops to muted. That is the thing a search route has which a\ngeneric \"pick one option\" control does not -- a try-order with a primary and\na safety net -- and it is now legible without reading the sentence under it.\n\nConfigured and observed routes can disagree, and that gap is what someone\ndebugging cost or latency actually cares about. The observed line now\ncompares its primary against the configured one and, on a mismatch, says the\nconfiguration has changed since and switches to the warn colour. A\nwebSearchConfiguredRoute() helper is the single source of truth for both the\nheadline and that comparison, so the two cannot drift into different\ndefinitions of \"the route\" as the manifest grows. No new endpoint, no new\nfield, no routing behaviour touched.\n\nSecondary provider cards are distinguished by presence rather than\nbrightness. They previously dropped to opacity 0.85, which dimmed the status\npill along with everything else -- exactly the information worth keeping\nsharp on a card you are about to compare against, since \"has a key\" is how\nyou judge which alternate you could switch to. Now only the effective card is\na raised surface; the others drop to the section's own background and are\nheld by their border alone, the way an inactive tab sits flush. Content stays\nat full contrast throughout, which also removes reduced-opacity text that\ncontrast guidance flags even when it is still readable. One line replaced\nthree, including a transition guard with nothing left to guard.\n\nAlso tidied inside websearch-owned CSS: masked key labels and provider meta\nuse --font-mono instead of a duplicated hardcoded stack, the advanced\ndisclosure summary became a small tracked label, and two rules still carried\nvar(--border, #333) / var(--panel, #1b1b1b) fallbacks from a palette this\nproject no longer has.\n\nThe static assertion in test_admin.py moved twice, both times deliberately:\n\"Configured route:\" copy genuinely went away, and the template string that\nbriefly replaced it stopped being real once the path became segmented spans.\nIt now asserts the two span classes exist, which checks the structure rather\nthan paraphrasing old copy.\n\nVerified: 953 tests pass with the leaked EXA_API_KEY unset, which also\nconfirms the six failures seen with it set are environmental. ruff, ty and\nnode --check clean. The class guard added in 4.35.2 covers the ws- prefix, so\nevery new class here was checked automatically for a matching rule.\n\nNot verified: no browser, so the flush de-emphasis, the weighted path and the\n320/560px breakpoints are reasoned from the CSS and DOM, not seen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
